# platform.linux.storage.sdcard

A virtual SD card in SPI mode, byte by byte: every byte the host clocks out is
answered by the byte a card would put on MISO, 0xFF when it has nothing to
say. It follows the SD Physical Layer Simplified Specification's SPI mode as
far as a driver can see:

- CMD0 with chip select low and a correct CRC enters SPI mode and answers
  idle; a CMD0 with a bad CRC is ignored, as a card ignores it.
- In the idle state only CMD0, CMD1, CMD8, CMD55, ACMD41, CMD58, and CMD59
  are legal; anything else is the illegal-command bit.
- CMD8 echoes the voltage and check pattern on a version 2 card and is
  illegal on a version 1 one. CMD55 then ACMD41 answers idle for two polls
  and ready on the third; an SDHC card asked without HCS stays idle.
- CMD58 reports CCS for an SDHC card; CMD9 and CMD10 send a CSD -- version 2
  for SDHC, version 1 with READ_BL_LEN 9 and C_SIZE_MULT 7 otherwise -- and a
  CID naming the emulator, each as a data block with its CRC16.
- CMD16 must be 512. CMD17 and CMD18 read, CMD18 streaming until CMD12;
  CMD24 and CMD25 write after the 0xFE or 0xFC token, CMD25 ending at the
  0xFD stop token, each block answered 0x05 and a few bytes of busy.
- CMD59 turns CRC checking on, after which a command with a bad CRC7 is
  answered with the CRC error bit and a data block with a bad CRC16 with
  0x0B, unwritten. CMD0 and CMD8 are always checked. CMD13 answers R2.
- An address past the end, or a byte address that is not a whole block on a
  standard-capacity card, is the address error bit.

Faults are part of the model, set from tests: the card removed (no answer at
all) and inserted (powered up afresh, its contents kept), write protection
(writes answered 0x0D, unwritten), a write failing after a number of blocks,
and the next read's CRC16 corrupted.

It uses only the standard library and the store, and builds on the host lane.
SDHC wants a size of whole 512 KiB; standard capacity whole 256 KiB, at most
1 GiB, which the version 1 CSD's fields can describe.
